('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Worth the extra $900? AMPD Bros Ace Mini vs Ace Adventure, two fat tyre e-bikes from the same Gold Coast brand. Ace Mini: $2,390, 720Wh, 65Nm, 37.5kg, 60km claimed range. Ace Adventure: $3,290, 960Wh, 80Nm, 35.0kg, 80km claimed. So the extra $900 buys 240Wh more battery, 15Nm more torque and a bike 2.5kg lighter. Claimed range is a best case figure, and both bikes are declared road-legal by the brand, so confirm with the seller. BIKE VS BIKE, from the eBikeFinder directory of 585 bikes. Compare them side by side: https://ebikefinder.au/compare?bikes=ampd-bros/ace-mini,ampd-bros/ace-adventure Which one would you buy? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-am-image.jpg'],'2026-10-14T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-15-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Worth the extra $900? AMPD Bros Ace Mini vs Ace Adventure$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$Worth the extra $900? AMPD Bros Ace Mini vs Ace Adventure, two fat tyre e-bikes from the same Gold Coast brand. Ace Mini: $2,390, 720Wh, 65Nm, 37.5kg, 60km claimed range. Ace Adventure: $3,290, 960Wh, 80Nm, 35.0kg, 80km claimed. So the extra $900 buys 240Wh more battery, 15Nm more torque and a bike 2.5kg lighter. Claimed range is a best case figure, and both bikes are declared road-legal by the brand, so confirm with the seller. BIKE VS BIKE, from the eBikeFinder directory of 585 bikes. Which one would you buy? Link in bio. #ebike #fattyre #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-am-image.jpg'],'2026-10-14T22:02:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-15-am-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Worth the extra $900? AMPD Bros Ace Mini vs Ace Adventure$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Do you actually need 100Nm? Only 1 in 5 e-bikes in the eBikeFinder directory has it. Of the 340 declared road-legal bikes that list torque: 35% are under 65Nm (119 bikes, median $2,995, mostly hub bikes), 46% sit at 65 to 99Nm (158 bikes, median $5,499, 139 of them mid-drive) and 19% reach 100Nm or more (63 bikes, median $8,500, all mid-drive, 36 of them trail e-MTBs). Our hills guide says to look for a mid-drive with 65Nm or more if you have serious climbs. For commuting on the flat, 100Nm is a bonus, not a need. Torque is the brand figure and prices are as listed in the directory. FINDER DATA, 585 bikes compared. Method: declared road-legal bikes with torque listed, banded by Nm, directory read 12 Oct 2026. Check torque on any bike: https://ebikefinder.au/ebikes What is the steepest hill on your ride? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-4.jpg'],'2026-10-15T07:30:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-15-pm-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Do you actually need 100Nm? Only 1 in 5 e-bikes has it.$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$Do you actually need 100Nm? Only 1 in 5 e-bikes in the eBikeFinder directory has it. Of the 340 declared road-legal bikes that list torque: 35% are under 65Nm (119 bikes, median $2,995, mostly hub bikes), 46% sit at 65 to 99Nm (158 bikes, median $5,499, 139 of them mid-drive) and 19% reach 100Nm or more (63 bikes, median $8,500, all mid-drive, 36 of them trail e-MTBs). Our hills guide says to look for a mid-drive with 65Nm or more if you have serious climbs. For commuting on the flat, 100Nm is a bonus, not a need. Torque is the brand figure and prices are as listed in the directory. FINDER DATA, 585 bikes compared. What is the steepest hill on your ride? Link in bio. #ebike #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder #torque$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-15-thu-pm-carousel-slide-4.jpg'],'2026-10-15T07:32:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-15-pm-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Do you actually need 100Nm? Only 1 in 5 e-bikes has it.$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$What $1,500 buys you. Of the 427 e-bikes in the eBikeFinder directory that the brand declares road-legal, 14 are in stock for under $1,500. The median one has a 360Wh battery, 43Nm of torque, a 24.5kg frame and a claimed 50km of range. Most torque: the Kona Coco HD and Dew HD at 60Nm, $1,499.99 at BikesOnline. Biggest battery: the Mate Go+ folding bike at 624Wh, $1,399 on sale at Electric Kicks. A typical pick: the Leitner Berlin Cruiser from $1,450. Claimed range is a best case figure, and compliance is brand-declared, so confirm with the seller. FINDER DATA, 585 bikes compared. Method: in stock, declared road-legal, listed under $1,500 in the directory (read 12 Oct 2026), prices on the three featured bikes re-checked on the retailer sites 12 Oct. Browse all: https://ebikefinder.au/ebikes Would you spend more or settle at $1,500? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-3.jpg'],'2026-10-15T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-16-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: What $1,500 buys you: 14 road-legal e-bikes in stock$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$What $1,500 buys you. Of the 427 e-bikes in the eBikeFinder directory that the brand declares road-legal, 14 are in stock for under $1,500. The median one has a 360Wh battery, 43Nm of torque, a 24.5kg frame and a claimed 50km of range. Most torque: the Kona Coco HD and Dew HD at 60Nm, $1,499.99 at BikesOnline. Biggest battery: the Mate Go+ folding bike at 624Wh, $1,399 on sale at Electric Kicks. A typical pick: the Leitner Berlin Cruiser from $1,450. Claimed range is a best case figure, and compliance is brand-declared, so confirm with the seller. FINDER DATA, 585 bikes compared. Would you spend more or settle at $1,500? Link in bio. #ebike #budgetebike #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-am-carousel-slide-3.jpg'],'2026-10-15T22:02:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-16-am-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: What $1,500 buys you: 14 road-legal e-bikes in stock$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Same motor. Different feel. The sensor is why. A cadence sensor only knows the pedals are turning, so power arrives a beat after you push and it ignores how hard you are working. It is common on budget bikes. A torque sensor measures how hard you push and matches the motor to it: push harder, get more help, ease off and it eases off. Most quality mid-drives use one. If you can, choose torque, and take a test ride before you pay. You feel the difference in the first hundred metres. Not every listing states the sensor type, so ask the seller. Full explainer: https://ebikefinder.au/guides/guide-torque-vs-cadence-sensor-ebikes Do you know which sensor your bike has? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-4.jpg'],'2026-10-16T07:30:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-16-pm-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Same motor. Different feel. The sensor is why.$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$Same motor. Different feel. The sensor is why. A cadence sensor only knows the pedals are turning, so power arrives a beat after you push and it ignores how hard you are working. It is common on budget bikes. A torque sensor measures how hard you push and matches the motor to it: push harder, get more help, ease off and it eases off. Most quality mid-drives use one. If you can, choose torque, and take a test ride before you pay. You feel the difference in the first hundred metres. Not every listing states the sensor type, so ask the seller. Do you know which sensor your bike has? Send this to someone shopping for an e-bike. Link in bio. #ebike #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder #ebiketips$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-16-fri-pm-carousel-slide-4.jpg'],'2026-10-16T07:32:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-16-pm-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Same motor. Different feel. The sensor is why.$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Where do you charge your e-bike? Fire and Rescue NSW calls lithium-ion batteries the fastest growing fire risk in NSW. Its advice: charge in an open area like a garage or shed, away from exits, on a hard surface such as concrete or tiles, never in or near bedrooms and living rooms, and keep a working smoke or heat alarm nearby. Do not charge while you sleep or are out, and unplug once it is full. Use only the charger that came with the bike, because a plug that fits is not a plug that is compatible. If a battery is swelling, leaking, cracked or overheating, never use or charge it again. Keep it outside, at least 3 metres from buildings, and never put it in the household bin. This is NSW advice, so check your own state fire service as well. More on batteries: https://ebikefinder.au/guides/guide-ebike-battery-fire-safety Where does your battery charge? Send this to someone who charges in the hallway. #ebikefinder #ebikesafety$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-am-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-am-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-am-carousel-slide-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-am-carousel-slide-4.jpg'],'2026-10-16T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-17-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Where do you charge your e-bike? Three rules from the fire service.$a$),
