('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Who is the AMPD Bros Riptide 3 actually for? Beach and town riders who want a classic cruiser look on 26 inch semi-fat tyres. $2,190 for the step-over (the step-through Riptide-S 3 is $2,690), with a 720Wh battery, 60Nm hub motor, hydraulic brakes, about 35kg and a claimed 60km of range. The catch: 60Nm is the mildest motor in the AMPD range and there is no suspension. Skip it if you ride sand or rough tracks, where the 4 inch tyres of the Chubbie 3 float better. Brand-declared road-legal, so order the road-compliant build and confirm with the seller. Claimed range is a best case figure. Full specs: https://ebikefinder.au/ebikes/ampd-bros/riptide-3 Cruiser or fat tyre, which one is you? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-pm-image.jpg'],'2026-10-17T07:30:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-17-pm-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Who is the Riptide 3 actually for? $2,190, 720Wh, 60Nm$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$Who is the AMPD Bros Riptide 3 actually for? Beach and town riders who want a classic cruiser look on 26 inch semi-fat tyres. $2,190 for the step-over (the step-through Riptide-S 3 is $2,690), with a 720Wh battery, 60Nm hub motor, hydraulic brakes, about 35kg and a claimed 60km of range. The catch: 60Nm is the mildest motor in the AMPD range and there is no suspension. Skip it if you ride sand or rough tracks, where the 4 inch tyres of the Chubbie 3 float better. Brand-declared road-legal, so order the road-compliant build and confirm with the seller. Claimed range is a best case figure. Cruiser or fat tyre, which one is you? Link in bio. #ebike #beachcruiser #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-17-sat-pm-image.jpg'],'2026-10-17T07:32:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-17-pm-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Who is the Riptide 3 actually for? $2,190, 720Wh, 60Nm$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$Why 250W doesn't mean slow. 250W is the continuous motor power limit for a road-legal e-bike in Australia, not a speed. Two things matter more. First, torque: across the declared road-legal bikes in the eBikeFinder directory the median mid-drive bike makes 85Nm and the median hub bike 45Nm, and our hills guide says to look for 65Nm or more if you have serious climbs. Second, the 25km/h cut out: assistance stops at 25km/h, but you can still pedal faster or coast downhill, and any throttle works only up to 6km/h. That combination keeps a bike legal in every state. Check your own state's rules before you ride. State by state rules: https://ebikefinder.au/ebike-laws What is the steepest hill you would want one for? #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-3.jpg'],'2026-10-17T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-18-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: 250W doesn't mean slow. Here is why.$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$Why 250W doesn't mean slow. 250W is the continuous motor power limit for a road-legal e-bike in Australia, not a speed. Two things matter more. First, torque: across the declared road-legal bikes in the eBikeFinder directory the median mid-drive bike makes 85Nm and the median hub bike 45Nm, and our hills guide says to look for 65Nm or more if you have serious climbs. Second, the 25km/h cut out: assistance stops at 25km/h, but you can still pedal faster or coast downhill, and any throttle works only up to 6km/h. That combination keeps a bike legal in every state. Check your own state's rules before you ride. What is the steepest hill you would want one for? Send this to a friend who thinks 250W is weak. Link in bio. #ebike #ebikeaustralia #ebikefinder #ebiketips$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-am-carousel-slide-3.jpg'],'2026-10-17T22:02:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-18-am-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: 250W doesn't mean slow. Here is why.$a$),
('982e3b99-127f-460b-95c6-d82a689efadc','image',$c$RETAILER RADAR: 4 shops, 146 prices. These are the shops most often behind a price in the eBikeFinder directory of 585 e-bikes. 99 Bikes (@99bikes) is behind 62, mostly Cube and Merida. Electric Bikes Brisbane (@electricbikesbrisbane) is behind 43, mostly Orbea and Kalkhoff. BikesOnline (@bikesonline.aus) is behind 23, led by Aventon, with Marin and Polygon. Leon Cycle (@leoncycleaustralia) is behind 18, all NCM. Own-brand stores such as AMPD Bros and Pedal are not counted here. Selling e-bikes in Australia and want a listing? Send us your range. Browse the full directory: https://ebikefinder.au/ebikes Which shop do you buy from? #ebikefinder #ebikeaustralia$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-pm-image.jpg'],'2026-10-18T07:30:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-18-pm-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: RETAILER RADAR: 4 shops, 146 prices in the 585 bike directory$a$),
('d11766da-8e25-43c6-9cea-1c2e81264272','image',$c$RETAILER RADAR: 4 shops, 146 prices. These are the shops most often behind a price in the eBikeFinder directory of 585 e-bikes. 99 Bikes (@99bikes) is behind 62, mostly Cube and Merida. Electric Bikes Brisbane (@electricbikesbrisbane) is behind 43, mostly Orbea and Kalkhoff. BikesOnline (@bikesonline.aus) is behind 23, led by Aventon, with Marin and Polygon. Leon Cycle (@leoncycleaustralia) is behind 18, all NCM. Own-brand stores such as AMPD Bros and Pedal are not counted here. Selling e-bikes in Australia and want a listing? Send us your range. Which shop do you buy from? Link in bio. @99bikes @electricbikesbrisbane @bikesonline.aus @leoncycleaustralia #ebike #ebikeaustralia #bikeshop #ebikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/ebikefinder/1013-2026-10-18-sun-pm-image.jpg'],'2026-10-18T07:32:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-ebikefinder-2026-10-18-pm-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: RETAILER RADAR: 4 shops, 146 prices in the 585 bike directory$a$),
('7a1b5eff-3026-4988-b5fd-5e42c8489b1c','image',$c$1,000g frame. 57mm tyres. Look has unveiled the G85 Cezal RS gravel race bike, built around a claimed 1,000g frame in a painted medium.

What changed: a high modulus carbon layup that saves 200g over the standard G85 Cezal. Look claims bottom bracket stiffness is up 19% and head tube stiffness up 20%.
Why it matters: tyre room is 57mm at the front and 50mm at the rear with a 1x drivetrain (45mm rear with 2x), but Look says it chose short 425mm chainstays and a snappy ride over maximum clearance.
Who should care: gravel racers who want a light frame with sharp handling.
Is it better? The weight and stiffness figures are Look's own. Complete bikes are listed at 8.3kg (SRAM Force XPLR) and 7.9kg (SRAM Red XPLR).

Pricing so far is in euros: frameset EUR 3,290, Force XPLR build EUR 6,990, Red XPLR build EUR 8,990. We have found no Australian price, stock or ship date yet, so ask a local Look stockist before planning around one.

Read our news post and compare gravel bikes sold in Australia: bikefinder.au/news

Would you pay race bike money for a lighter frame? #gravelbike #bikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-01-look-cezal-rs.jpg'],'2026-10-12T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-bikefinder-2026-10-13-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: 1,000g frame. 57mm tyres.$a$),
('32a573bb-697e-4c12-b64a-290a5991bbfc','image',$c$1,000g frame. 57mm tyres. Look has unveiled the G85 Cezal RS, a gravel race bike built around a claimed 1,000g frame (medium, painted).

What changed: a high modulus carbon layup that saves 200g over the standard G85 Cezal, with bottom bracket stiffness up 19% and head tube stiffness up 20%. Geometry is shared with the standard bike, including 425mm chainstays.
Tyre room: 57mm front and 50mm rear with a 1x drivetrain, or 45mm rear with 2x.
Who should care: gravel racers who want a light, sharp handling frame rather than maximum tyre room.
Is it better? The weight and stiffness numbers are Look's own claims. Complete bikes are 8.3kg (Force XPLR) and 7.9kg (Red XPLR).

Pricing so far is in euros: frameset EUR 3,290, Force build EUR 6,990, Red build EUR 8,990. No Australian price or date yet.

