#aroundthebay #roadbike #endurancebike #cycling #bikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-09-bay-1.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-09-bay-2.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-09-bay-3.jpg','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-09-bay-4.jpg'],'2026-10-16T22:02:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-bikefinder-2026-10-17-am-instagram',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: 220km on Sunday. Which bike?$a$),
('7a1b5eff-3026-4988-b5fd-5e42c8489b1c','image',$c$Where gravel ends: the low gear. The Trek Marlin 7 hardtail ($1,499.99) has a lowest gear ratio of 0.60. The Kona Sutra gravel bike ($3,499) has 0.79. Across the 31 gravel bikes in BikeFinder's Gravel Finder that list a ratio, the average is 0.88.

Lower is easier uphill. The Marlin's 0.60 comes from a 30 tooth chainring and an 11 to 50 tooth 12 speed cassette, a mountain bike setup. That is 32% lower than the gravel average.

The trade runs the other way at the top: the Sutra's highest gear is 4.18 and the Marlin's is 2.73, so the gravel bike rolls faster on flat tarmac.

For gravel riders who climb steep loose tracks, it is worth checking the low gear before you buy. Ratios are derived from the chainring and cassette on each bike page and describe the gearing, not any rider's ability.

Method: BikeFinder bike pages and Gravel Finder, checked 12 October 2026. Researched specs, not ride tests. Prices are last seen AUD listings.

Filter gravel bikes by gearing at bikefinder.au/gravel

Do you have a low enough gear? #gravelbike #bikefinder$c$,ARRAY['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media/15da38d/posts/2026-10-13/bikefinder/1013-bf-11-low-gear.jpg'],'2026-10-17T22:00:00+00','queued','roxy','r1013-bikefinder-2026-10-18-am-facebook',$a$Roxy preflight 12 Oct: full size contact sheet audit of every slide, sources per manifest (verified 12 Oct), white bg, no dashes, no repeat of 2026-09-28/10-05 manifests. Hook: Where gravel ends: the low gear.$a$),
('32a573bb-697e-4c12-b64a-290a5991bbfc','image',$c$Where gravel ends: the low gear. The Trek Marlin 7 hardtail ($1,499.99) has a lowest gear ratio of 0.60. The Kona Sutra gravel bike ($3,499) has 0.79. The average of the 31 gravel bikes in our Gravel Finder that list a ratio is 0.88.

Lower is easier uphill. A 0.60 gear is 32% lower than the gravel average. It comes from a 30 tooth chainring and an 11 to 50 tooth cassette, which is a mountain bike setup.

That is one of the cleaner lines between gravel and mountain bikes. Gravel gearing tops out higher for road speed (the Sutra's top gear is 4.18, the Marlin's 2.73), so it climbs less easily and rolls faster on flat tarmac.

Ratios come from the chainring and cassette on each bike page. They describe the gearing, not any rider's ability.

Method: BikeFinder bike pages and Gravel Finder, checked 12 Oct 2026. Researched specs, not ride tests.

